/* lode's app-level Textual stylesheet (lode-1i8.4).
 *
 * Before this file the repo had no TUI CSS at all -- every screen rendered
 * with Textual's bare unstyled layout. Most visibly,
 * :class:`~lode.tui.screens.discard_confirm.DiscardConfirmScreen`'s
 * Save/Discard/Cancel prompt had no sizing or centering, so it read as a
 * blank full screen with a floating line of text rather than a popup dialog.
 *
 * :class:`~textual.screen.ModalScreen`'s own ``DEFAULT_CSS`` already dims the
 * screen underneath (``background: $background 60%``) -- confirmed against
 * the installed Textual version (8.2.8) before writing this file, per the
 * ticket's design note -- so the only gap this file closes is sizing and
 * centering the dialog box itself; the editor stays visible (dimmed) behind
 * it for free.
 *
 * Loaded via ``LodeApp.CSS_PATH`` (:mod:`lode.tui.app`) as an *external*
 * stylesheet (not ``DEFAULT_CSS``) -- this file is the repo's first, and the
 * pattern every future screen's styling should extend rather than
 * duplicate. It ships with the package via
 * ``[tool.setuptools.package-data]`` in ``pyproject.toml``.
 */

/* All the modal dialogs sharing the .confirm-dialog frame below (lode-1ip2,
 * lode-f0qf) -- DiscardConfirmScreen, EnrichmentModalScreen, the three
 * YesNoConfirmScreen subclasses (DeleteConfirmScreen,
 * NoEgressClearConfirmScreen, SaveAsNoteConfirmScreen),
 * RelatedNoteModalScreen, SnapshotViewerScreen and HelpScreen -- are
 * collapsed onto one comma-separated selector so centering has ONE
 * definition too, not eight near-identical type-selector copies. */
DiscardConfirmScreen,
EnrichmentModalScreen,
YesNoConfirmScreen,
RelatedNoteModalScreen,
SnapshotViewerScreen,
HelpScreen {
    align: center middle;
}

/* Together with .confirm-dialog below, this is what closes lode-a50f's
 * defect properly: a new dialog's outer container is centered (via the type
 * selector above), framed AND sized (via the class below) with no tcss of
 * its own, so it cannot ship as the bare unframed line at the top-left that
 * NoEgressClearConfirmScreen did, and cannot ship as a full-screen box
 * either (Textual's Vertical default is width: 1fr; height: 1fr, which is
 * what a size-less dialog would otherwise inherit). An id rule below is now
 * an OPT-IN for a dialog that genuinely deviates from the small single-line
 * default -- not an obligation a new dialog can forget. */

/* The default: a small, fixed-size popup whose prompt is a single line --
 * delete (lode-d32.1), no-egress CLEAR (lode-a50f) and the capture
 * Save/Discard/Cancel confirm (lode-0wj.1, #capture-confirm-dialog) all take
 * it as-is. */
.confirm-dialog {
    width: 50;
    height: auto;
    border: thick $primary;
    background: $panel;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* The larger, scrollable popup size shared by the enrichment inspector
 * (lode-ay5.2), save-as-note confirm (lode-35nu.11.4), the related-note
 * modal (lode-olmi.9), the content-viewer modal (lode-0sjj) and the
 * keybinding help overlay (lode-2bt3.2) -- each note/answer/binding list can
 * run longer than the terminal height. Height is bounded (not auto) so the
 * container itself -- not the dialog growing past the screen -- is what
 * scrolls. An id selector outranks the .confirm-dialog class, so this
 * overrides the default size above while still inheriting its frame. */
#enrichment-inspector-dialog,
#save-as-note-confirm-dialog,
#related-note-modal-dialog,
#snapshot-viewer-dialog,
#help-dialog {
    width: 80%;
    height: 80%;
}

/* Related-notes panel (lode-35nu.10) -- INVARIANT: exactly one row per note,
 * at any terminal width. RelatedNotesPanel.on_mount pins a fixed height of
 * related_notes_limit + header + focus-border rows, and that arithmetic is
 * exact only while no note line wraps; delete these two declarations and the
 * tail of the list silently goes off screen. Full reasoning, the 80x24
 * measurement, and the rejected alternatives: docs/tui.md. */
RelatedNotesPanel {
    text-wrap: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

/* Related-notes panel (lode-olmi.9) -- a visible border once Ctrl+F moves
 * focus onto it, so "you can now step through results" has an on-screen cue
 * rather than relying purely on the in-text "▸" selection marker. */
RelatedNotesPanel:focus {
    border: round $accent;
}

/* Tags screen (lode-olmi.6) -- top panel (tag multi-select) is capped to 30%
 * of the available height so the bottom panel (the filtered notes list)
 * keeps most of the vertical space; each panel scrolls its own overflow
 * independently. This is a deliberate override of the blanket DataTable
 * rule below -- #tags-tag-list is itself a DataTable, and the ID selector's
 * higher specificity wins over the element selector regardless of rule
 * order. */
#tags-tag-list {
    height: 30%;
    border: solid $primary;
}

/* Keybinding help overlay (lode-2bt3.2). BindingsTable's own component-class
 * styling (key/description/divider/header) is normally supplied by
 * Textual's KeyPanel wrapper (textual/widgets/_key_panel.py); reproduced
 * here directly since HelpScreen composes BindingsTable on its own, not via
 * KeyPanel -- same declarations KeyPanel's own DEFAULT_CSS uses. Centering
 * and #help-dialog's frame/size are covered by the shared rules above. */
HelpScreen BindingsTable > .bindings-table--key {
    color: $text-accent;
    text-style: bold;
    padding: 0 1;
}

HelpScreen BindingsTable > .bindings-table--description {
    color: $foreground;
}

HelpScreen BindingsTable > .bindings-table--divider {
    color: transparent;
}

HelpScreen BindingsTable > .bindings-table--header {
    color: $text-primary;
    text-style: underline;
}

/* Every DataTable gets a bounded height structurally (lode-efn2), rather
 * than requiring each screen's author to remember a per-id 1fr rule -- that
 * per-id mechanism had to be applied twice, each time only after a bug
 * report (#browse-table/lode-juz8.2, #config-knobs/lode-l38d.2).
 *
 * WHY A TABLE NEEDS THIS AT ALL (measured against textual 8.2.8 during
 * lode-efn2's review -- the story this comment used to tell was wrong).
 * DataTable's own DEFAULT_CSS is `height: auto; max-height: 100%`, and that
 * `100%` resolves against the PARENT's height -- NOT against the space left
 * over after the parent's other children. So an unconstrained table
 * over-claims whenever it has a space-consuming sibling: #config-knobs
 * renders 7 rows PAST the docked Footer (it shares a Vertical with a 7-row
 * Static), and #browse-table pushes its non-docked search Input below the
 * fold. Those rows are UNREACHABLE, not merely scrolled-away -- nothing here
 * is a scroll container, and Screen.max_scroll_y stays 0 (corrected
 * diagnosis from lode-l38d.2's review, which had first mis-described this as
 * "the screen scrolls"). `1fr` fixes it because fr resolves against the
 * space REMAINING after siblings; DataTable is a ScrollView, so it then
 * scrolls its own rows internally.
 *
 * Note version-history-table and external-picker-table are each their
 * Screen's SOLE non-docked child, so max-height 100% already bounded them
 * exactly -- they never overflowed, and lode-efn2 was filed believing
 * otherwise. They are covered here for uniformity and to survive a future
 * restructure, not to fix a live bug.
 *
 * Re-verified safe as of lode-efn2: all six DataTables in this app live in a
 * plain, non-modal Screen's layout -- none of the eight ModalScreen dialogs
 * above contains one, so this rule never reaches into an auto/fixed-size
 * dialog box. Re-check that if a DataTable is ever added inside one. Screens
 * that genuinely differ (like #tags-tag-list above) keep an explicit per-id
 * override. Full mechanism, the per-widget DEFAULT_CSS table, and the
 * convention for non-DataTable panels: docs/tui.md. */
DataTable {
    height: 1fr;
}
